# session/include -- the ANCHOR list: windows session-mgr keeps placing even in
# the CONSERVATIVE steady state.
#
# session-mgr places AGGRESSIVELY for the first few minutes after login (or a
# `session-mgr aggressive` kick) -- it puts every known window back as it opens,
# which is what lets Chrome fire up and settle into place. Once it ages into
# STEADY state it stops teleporting: a window you reopen just appears where you
# are and STAYS. This file is the opt-in exception -- the few windows you want
# snapped back to their remembered workspace even in steady state.
#
# Default is FOLLOW-ME: an empty file anchors nothing, so nothing gets dragged
# around mid-session. Add entries only for windows you genuinely want pinned.
#
# One rule per line, same shape as session/exclude:
#
#   <app-regex> :: <title-regex>
#
# BOTH fields required. Each is a Python regex matched with re.search
# (UNANCHORED -- a substring test); use '.*' for "any", add ^...$ to pin.
# Lines starting with # are comments; blanks ignored. exclude still WINS: a
# window matching both is never placed. Edits apply live (the watcher
# reloads on save). `session-mgr include` shows the rules as loaded.
#
# Examples (commented -- uncomment/adapt as you find your anchors). Prefer a
# unique app_id (an app or a Chrome --app/PWA) over a shared-app title, which
# churns with tabs:
#   A messaging app -- always snap back to its workspace:
# Slack :: .*
#   A Gmail PWA window (distinct app_id from generic chrome):
# .*[Gg]mail.* :: .*
